# Why fatty soap makes scum in hard water
#
# This uses a declared sodium-stearate-equivalent teaching reagent, not dish
# detergent and not a claim about any branded soap. Two fatty-carboxylate
# equivalents bind each calcium or magnesium ion; aggregate mass follows the
# actually consumed sodium soap and Ca/Mg, with released sodium returned to
# solution.
register lv2

# Soft-water control: no Ca2+ or Mg2+, so no aggregate is available.
add v1 water 100g
add v1 fatty_soap 1g
look v1

# Hard-water test: calcium is the limiting reagent and produces conserved
# soap-scum aggregate plus released sodium ions.
new beaker
add v2 water 100g
add v2 CaCl2 0.001mol
add v2 fatty_soap 1g
look v2

# Magnesium follows the same two-equivalents-per-ion rule.
new beaker
add v3 water 100g
add v3 MgSO4 0.001mol
add v3 fatty_soap 1g
look v3

# Foam/lather, micelles, chain-length mixtures, builders and carbonate scale
# are not inferred from this bounded precipitation calculation.
